<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch10-page1-replicator-system" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Replicator System</title>

  <p>
    In this chapter, we continue to study evolutionary games, so we are
    dealing with a large population whose members play a symmetric two-player game against each other over and
    over. In Chapter 8, the population state <m>\sigma</m> was fixed, and we
    asked whether it was evolutionarily stable, that is, whether it could
    hold off a small group of invaders.
  </p>
  <p>
    In this chapter, we model the population state as changing continuously
    over time. We will write the population state as a function of time:
    <me>
      \sigma(t) = p_1(t)s_1 + p_2(t)s_2 + \cdots + p_n(t)s_n,
    </me>
    where <m>p_i(t)</m> is the fraction of the population using strategy
    <m>s_i</m> at time <m>t</m>. The state at the start is
    <m>\sigma(0)</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
    This makes differential equations a natural model, and we can use the
    ideas from Chapter 9 to analyze their solutions and equilibria. If a
    strategy does well, you'd expect the portion of the population using it
    to increase with time. On the other hand, a strategy that doesn't do
    well should die out! We will look for asymptotically stable equilibria
    that the population state approaches.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Reading 1: Section 10.1                                      -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <assemblage xml:id="read-10-1">
    <title>Section 10.1, Replicator System</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 10.1.</alert> 
    </p>
    <p>
      In the reading, the following important ideas are introduced:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            The <term>replicator system</term> (10.1) is the system of
            differential equations that models how the population state
            changes:
            <me>
              \dot{p}_i = \bigl(\pi_1(s_i, \sigma) - \pi_1(\sigma, \sigma)\bigr)p_i,
              \qquad i = 1, \ldots, n.
            </me>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Theorem 10.1 lists some basic properties of the replicator
            system, about which sets of population states solutions stay in
            (<term>invariance</term>) and which states are always
            equilibria.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      Reading the proof of Theorem 10.1
      will help you get acquainted with the concepts and notation, but it is
      not strictly required to get through the material.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Let's write down the important ideas from the reading for reference.
    We will use all of them throughout this chapter.
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="para-ch10-replicator-equation">
    <title>The replicator system</title>
    <p>
      Here is the replicator system again, with the different parts labeled. (Note that in the replicator system,
      <m>\sigma</m>, <m>p_i</m>, and <m>\dot{p}_i</m> are all functions of
      time.)
      <me>
        \underbrace{\dot{p}_i}_{\substack{\text{rate of change of}\\ \text{the share using } s_i}}
        \;=\;
        \underbrace{\bigl(\pi_1(s_i, \sigma) - \pi_1(\sigma, \sigma)\bigr)}_{\substack{\text{how much better } s_i \text{ does}\\ \text{than the population average}}}
        \;
        \underbrace{p_i}_{\substack{\text{current share}\\ \text{using } s_i}}
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Recall that <m>\pi_1(s_i, \sigma)</m> is the expected payoff to an
      individual using <m>s_i</m> against a random member of the population,
      and <m>\pi_1(\sigma, \sigma)</m> is the average payoff in the
      population. Both are computed using the <em>current</em> state
      <m>\sigma = \sigma(t)</m>, so as the population changes, the payoffs
      change too. 
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <exercise xml:id="check-replicator-signs">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Using the labeled replicator system, which of the following
        statements are true? <alert>Select all that apply.</alert>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no" multiple-correct="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>s_i</m> does better than average, <m>p_i</m> increases.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            True. The payoff advantage is positive and <m>p_i \gt 0</m>, so
            <m>\dot{p}_i \gt 0</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>s_i</m> does worse than average, <m>p_i</m> increases.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            False. The payoff advantage is negative, so
            <m>\dot{p}_i \lt 0</m> and <m>p_i</m> decreases.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>s_i</m> does exactly as well as average, or nobody uses it,
            <m>p_i</m> stays constant.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            True. In the first case the payoff advantage is 0, and in the
            second case <m>p_i = 0</m>. Either way, <m>\dot{p}_i = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    The book gives two ways to interpret this. In biology, strategies with
    higher payoffs have more offspring. In social situations, people see
    which strategies are succeeding and copy them.
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="para-ch10-simplex">
    <title>The simplex and its strata</title>
    <p>
      The replicator system only makes sense for population states, where
      every share is nonnegative and the shares add up to 1. This set is
      called the <term>simplex</term>:
      <me>
        \Sigma = \Bigl\{(p_1, \ldots, p_n) : \text{all } p_i \geq 0 \text{ and } \sum p_i = 1\Bigr\}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      The simplex is split into pieces called <term>strata</term> based on
      which strategies are actually in use. For a nonempty set
      <m>I \subseteq \{1, \ldots, n\}</m>, the stratum <m>\Sigma_I</m>
      contains the states where exactly the strategies with
      <m>i \in I</m> are used:
      <me>
        \Sigma_I = \Bigl\{(p_1, \ldots, p_n) : p_i \gt 0 \text{ if } i \in I,\ p_i = 0 \text{ if } i \notin I, \text{ and } \sum p_i = 1\Bigr\}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Figure 10.1 gives a good visualization of the different strata for
      <m>n = 3</m>.
    </p>
    
  </paragraphs>
  <p>
    Last, we restate Theorem 10.1 for reference, and then we'll see a quick
    interpretation of each part!
  </p>
  <theorem xml:id="thm-10-1-restated">
    <title>Theorem 10.1</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The replicator system has the following properties:
        <ol marker="(1)">
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>p_i = 0</m>, then <m>\dot{p}_i = 0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>S(p_1, \ldots, p_n) = \sum p_i</m>. If <m>S = 1</m>,
              then <m>\dot{S} = 0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Each stratum <m>\Sigma_I</m> is invariant.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Each stratum <m>\Sigma_{\{i\}}</m> is an equilibrium.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    Here are ways to think about each part of the theorem:
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>(1)</alert> An inactive strategy stays inactive. The model
          has no mutation, so a new strategy can never appear on its own.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>(2)</alert> The fractions of the population using each
          strategy always add up to 1. It's good that this is true!
          Otherwise, <m>\sigma(t)</m> would stop being a legitimate
          population state.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>(3)</alert> A solution never leaves the stratum it starts
          in. In particular, an active strategy never dies out completely in
          finite time. Its share can <em>approach</em> 0 as
          <m>t \to \infty</m>, but it never reaches 0.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <alert>(4)</alert> Note that <m>\Sigma_{\{i\}}</m> consists of just
          the pure state <m>s_i</m>. So this says every pure state, where the
          whole population uses one strategy, is an equilibrium. That is,
          if the initial population state uses only this pure strategy, it
          stays that way for all time! However, <alert>an equilibrium does
          not have to be stable</alert>: nearby solutions could move away
          from it. Deciding which equilibria are stable is a big part of
          this chapter.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="para-ch10-reduction">
    <title>Reducing the number of equations</title>
    <p>
      The end of Section 10.1 points out that we don't need all
      <m>n</m> equations. Since the shares add up to 1, we can use
      <m>p_n = 1 - p_1 - \cdots - p_{n-1}</m> and keep only the first
      <m>n - 1</m> equations. We will see this in the Microsoft vs. Apple
      example coming up!
    </p>
  
  </paragraphs>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Example: Microsoft vs. Apple                                 -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <p>
    In Section 10.2, the book discusses an example: Microsoft vs. Apple. I
    have broken down the math in the following example block, with some
    dropdown solutions and checkpoint questions. You can read it in the book
    first if you like, but this breaks down the different steps, and some
    parts will have you thinking more actively about the example!
  </p>
  <example xml:id="eg-microsoft-apple">
    <title>Microsoft vs. Apple</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
       In the early days of personal computers, you could buy a
        computer running Microsoft Windows or one running Apple's operating
        system. Apple's was better, but neither handled files from the other
        well, so if you and your coworker used different systems, not much
        got done. The strategies are buy Microsoft (<m>m</m>) or buy Apple
        (<m>a</m>):
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-ch10-microsoft-apple">
        <title>Microsoft vs. Apple</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>m</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>a</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>m</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(1, 1)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(2, 2)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        This is case (3) of Theorem 8.5 (a game like Stag Hunt). As the book
        lists, there are three Nash equilibria:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>(m, m)</m> and <m>(a, a)</m>, which are strict, so the
              population states <m>m</m> and <m>a</m> are evolutionarily
              stable;
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>(\sigma^*, \sigma^*)</m> with
              <m>\sigma^* = \frac{2}{3}m + \frac{1}{3}a</m>, which is not
              evolutionarily stable.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        Now let's see what the replicator system says. Write the population
        state as <m>\sigma = p_1 m + p_2 a</m>, so <m>p_1</m> is the share
        using Microsoft and <m>p_2</m> is the share using Apple.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="eg-ms-apple-payoffs">
      <title>The payoffs</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\pi_1(m, \sigma)</m>, <m>\pi_1(a, \sigma)</m>, and
          <m>\pi_1(\sigma, \sigma)</m> in terms of <m>p_1</m> and
          <m>p_2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          A Microsoft user gets 1 against another Microsoft user and 0
          against an Apple user, so
          <me>
            \pi_1(m, \sigma) = p_1 \cdot 1 + p_2 \cdot 0 = p_1.
          </me>
          An Apple user gets 0 against a Microsoft user and 2 against
          another Apple user, so
          <me>
            \pi_1(a, \sigma) = p_1 \cdot 0 + p_2 \cdot 2 = 2p_2.
          </me>
          The average payoff weights each strategy's payoff by its share:
          <me>
            \pi_1(\sigma, \sigma) = p_1 \pi_1(m, \sigma) + p_2 \pi_1(a, \sigma) = p_1^2 + 2p_2^2.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="eg-ms-apple-p1dot">
      <title>The equation for <m>\dot{p}_1</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the replicator equation for <m>\dot{p}_1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Plug the payoffs into
          <m>\dot{p}_1 = \bigl(\pi_1(m, \sigma) - \pi_1(\sigma, \sigma)\bigr)p_1</m>:
          <me>
            \dot{p}_1 = \bigl(p_1 - (p_1^2 + 2p_2^2)\bigr)p_1.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="check-ms-apple-p2dot">
      <title>The equation for <m>\dot{p}_2</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now try it yourself! Which of the following is the replicator
          equation for <m>\dot{p}_2</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><m>\dot{p}_2 = \bigl(2p_2 - (p_1^2 + 2p_2^2)\bigr)p_2</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Right. This is
              <m>\bigl(\pi_1(a, \sigma) - \pi_1(\sigma, \sigma)\bigr)p_2</m>,
              with <m>\pi_1(a, \sigma) = 2p_2</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>\dot{p}_2 = 2p_2 - (p_1^2 + 2p_2^2)</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. This is the payoff advantage of <m>a</m>, but the
              replicator equation multiplies it by the current share
              <m>p_2</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>\dot{p}_2 = \bigl(p_1 - (p_1^2 + 2p_2^2)\bigr)p_2</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. This uses <m>\pi_1(m, \sigma) = p_1</m>, the payoff
              to a Microsoft user. For <m>\dot{p}_2</m>, we need the payoff
              to an Apple user, <m>\pi_1(a, \sigma) = 2p_2</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>\dot{p}_2 = \bigl((p_1^2 + 2p_2^2) - 2p_2\bigr)p_2</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. The subtraction is backward: this would make Apple
              shrink when it does better than average. The replicator
              equation uses the strategy's payoff <em>minus</em> the
              average.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="eg-ms-apple-reduce">
      <title>Reducing to one variable</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          As we noted after Theorem 10.1, we only need one of these
          equations: since <m>p_2 = 1 - p_1</m>, knowing <m>p_1(t)</m> tells
          us <m>p_2(t)</m> too. Substitute <m>p_2 = 1 - p_1</m> into the
          equation for <m>\dot{p}_1</m> and factor it.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Start with the equation for <m>\dot{p}_1</m> and substitute
          <m>p_2 = 1 - p_1</m>:
          <me>
            \dot{p}_1 = \bigl(p_1 - p_1^2 - 2p_2^2\bigr)p_1
            = \bigl(p_1 - p_1^2 - 2\underbrace{(1 - p_1)^2}_{p_2 = 1 - p_1}\bigr)p_1.
          </me>
          We know in advance that <m>1 - p_1</m> must be a factor, since
          <m>p_1 = 1</m> is an equilibrium. To pull it out, notice that
          <m>p_1 - p_1^2 = (1 - p_1)p_1</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>\dot{p}_1 \amp = \bigl((1 - p_1)p_1 - 2(1 - p_1)^2\bigr)p_1</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = (1 - p_1)\bigl(p_1 - 2(1 - p_1)\bigr)p_1</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = p_1(1 - p_1)(3p_1 - 2).</mrow>
          </md>
          So the whole replicator system comes down to one autonomous
          differential equation,
          <me>
            \dot{p}_1 = p_1(1 - p_1)(3p_1 - 2).
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="check-ms-apple-equilibria">
      <title>The equilibria</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now let's find all the equilibria and draw the whole phase line.
        </p>
        <p>
          Which values of <m>p_1</m> in <m>[0, 1]</m> are equilibria of
          <m>\dot{p}_1 = p_1(1 - p_1)(3p_1 - 2)</m>?
          <alert>Select all that apply.</alert>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><m>p_1 = 0</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Yes. This is the factor <m>p_1</m>, and it is the pure state
              where everyone uses Apple, which Theorem 10.1(4) says is
              always an equilibrium.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>p_1 = \frac{1}{3}</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              No. At <m>p_1 = \frac{1}{3}</m>, the factor
              <m>3p_1 - 2 = -1</m>, so <m>\dot{p}_1 \neq 0</m>. (The
              equilibrium <m>\sigma^*</m> has <m>p_2 = \frac{1}{3}</m>, not
              <m>p_1</m>.)
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>p_1 = \frac{1}{2}</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              No. At <m>p_1 = \frac{1}{2}</m>, the factor
              <m>3p_1 - 2 = -\frac{1}{2}</m>, so <m>\dot{p}_1 \neq 0</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><m>p_1 = \frac{2}{3}</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Yes. This is where <m>3p_1 - 2 = 0</m>. It is also where both
              strategies earn the same payoff:
              <m>\pi_1(m, \sigma) = p_1 = \frac{2}{3}</m> and
              <m>\pi_1(a, \sigma) = 2p_2 = \frac{2}{3}</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><m>p_1 = 1</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Yes. This is the factor <m>1 - p_1</m>, and it is the pure
              state where everyone uses Microsoft.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="check-ms-apple-phase-line">
      <title>The phase line</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which is the phase line for
          <m>\dot{p}_1 = p_1(1 - p_1)(3p_1 - 2)</m> on <m>[0, 1]</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\underset{0}{\bullet} \longleftarrow\!\!\longleftarrow \underset{2/3}{\bullet} \longrightarrow\!\!\longrightarrow \underset{1}{\bullet}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Right. For <m>0 \lt p_1 \lt \frac{2}{3}</m>, the factors
              <m>p_1</m> and <m>1 - p_1</m> are positive and
              <m>3p_1 - 2</m> is negative, so <m>\dot{p}_1 \lt 0</m> and the
              arrows point left. For <m>\frac{2}{3} \lt p_1 \lt 1</m>, all
              three factors are positive, so the arrows point right.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\underset{0}{\bullet} \longrightarrow\!\!\longrightarrow \underset{2/3}{\bullet} \longleftarrow\!\!\longleftarrow \underset{1}{\bullet}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. The arrows are backward. For
              <m>0 \lt p_1 \lt \frac{2}{3}</m>, the factor <m>3p_1 - 2</m>
              is negative, so <m>\dot{p}_1 \lt 0</m> and the arrows point
              left.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\underset{0}{\bullet} \longrightarrow\!\!\longrightarrow \underset{2/3}{\bullet} \longrightarrow\!\!\longrightarrow \underset{1}{\bullet}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. The sign of <m>\dot{p}_1</m> changes at
              <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>, because the factor <m>3p_1 - 2</m> changes
              sign there: it is negative below <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\underset{0}{\bullet} \longleftarrow\!\!\longleftarrow \underset{2/3}{\bullet} \longleftarrow\!\!\longleftarrow \underset{1}{\bullet}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. The sign of <m>\dot{p}_1</m> changes at
              <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>, because the factor <m>3p_1 - 2</m> changes
              sign there: it is positive above <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        From the phase line, we can read off the stability of each
        equilibrium:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>p_1 = 0</m> (everyone uses Apple) is stable.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>p_1 = 1</m> (everyone uses Microsoft) is
              stable.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>p_1 = \frac{2}{3}</m> is unstable. It is a
              tipping point between the other two.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        You will be asked to interpret the equilibria and their stability in
        the checkpoints after the reading!
      </p>
      <p>
        Compare this with the game analysis at the start of the example. The
        three equilibria of the replicator system match the three symmetric
        Nash equilibria exactly: <m>p_1 = 1</m> is <m>(m, m)</m>,
        <m>p_1 = 0</m> is <m>(a, a)</m>, and <m>p_1 = \frac{2}{3}</m> is
        <m>(\sigma^*, \sigma^*)</m>. Even better, <alert>the stable equilibria
        are exactly the evolutionarily stable states</alert> <m>m</m> and
        <m>a</m>, and the unstable one is <m>\sigma^*</m>, which is not
        evolutionarily stable. We will see later that this is
        not a coincidence!
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </example>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Reading 2: Section 10.2                                      -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <assemblage xml:id="read-10-2">
      <title>Section 10.2, Microsoft vs. Apple</title>
      <p>
        <alert>Read Section 10.2.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        We already did the math in the example above, so you can skim over the
        computations. Read it for the full context: the book's discussion of
        the game, Figure 10.2, and why the worse computer can still take
        over.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
      Now let's make sure you can correctly interpret the equilibria and
      their stability!
    </p>
    <exercise xml:id="check-ms-apple-initial-share">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For which initial Microsoft shares <m>p_1(0)</m> does the
          population eventually end up with everyone using Microsoft?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>p_1(0) \gt \frac{1}{2}</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. A Microsoft majority is not enough. For every
              <m>p_1</m> between <m>0</m> and <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>, the
              Microsoft share shrinks.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>p_1(0) \geq \frac{2}{3}</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Close, but check the endpoint. If <m>p_1(0) = \frac{2}{3}</m>,
              the population starts at an equilibrium, so it stays at
              <m>\frac{2}{3}</m> forever.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><m>p_1(0) \gt \frac{2}{3}</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Right. This is the basin of attraction of <m>p_1 = 1</m>. Any
              population that starts with more than <m>\frac{2}{3}</m> using
              Microsoft ends up all Microsoft, even though everyone would be
              better off with Apple. Similarly, <m>[0, \frac{2}{3})</m> is the
              basin of attraction of <m>p_1 = 0</m>: starting with fewer than
              <m>\frac{2}{3}</m> using Microsoft, everyone ends up with
              Apple.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><m>p_1(0) \gt \frac{1}{3}</m></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. The tipping point is at <m>p_1 = \frac{2}{3}</m>,
              not <m>\frac{1}{3}</m>. (It is <m>p_2</m> that equals
              <m>\frac{1}{3}</m> there.)
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="check-ms-apple-start-at-sigma-star">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose the population starts at
          <m>\sigma(0) = \frac{2}{3}m + \frac{1}{3}a</m>, so
          <m>p_1(0) = \frac{2}{3}</m>. How does the population evolve over
          time?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>It stays at <m>p_1 = \frac{2}{3}</m> forever.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Right. <m>p_1 = \frac{2}{3}</m> is an equilibrium, so
              <m>\dot{p}_1 = 0</m> there and the population never moves. It
              is unstable, but that only matters if the population starts
              <em>near</em> <m>\frac{2}{3}</m> rather than exactly at it.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>It moves toward everyone using Microsoft.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. That happens if the population starts
              <em>above</em> <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>. Starting exactly at the
              equilibrium, <m>\dot{p}_1 = 0</m>, so nothing changes.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>It moves toward everyone using Apple.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not quite. That happens if the population starts
              <em>below</em> <m>\frac{2}{3}</m>. Starting exactly at the
              equilibrium, <m>\dot{p}_1 = 0</m>, so nothing changes.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <remark xml:id="remark-ms-apple-mystery">
      <p>
        The book calls the mixed Nash equilibrium
        <m>\sigma^* = \frac{2}{3}m + \frac{1}{3}a</m> a <q>mystery</q>: why
        would anyone choose the worse computer with higher probability? Because
        <m>\sigma^*</m> is unstable, it is not a state the population is
        likely to settle into. It is better thought of as the
        tipping point between the two basins of attraction.
        Since Apple users get twice the payoff when they match, Microsoft
        needs more than <m>\frac{2}{3}</m> of the population before it does
        better than Apple. Any small deviation from <m>\sigma^*</m> sends
        the population all the way to everyone using Microsoft or everyone
        using Apple.
      </p>
    </remark>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-10-1" iframe="question-form.html?section=10.1" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
